    "narrative": "For LawDog\u2019s 2026-07-07 slate, our optimal lineup is anchored by **Taylor Ward** (66.84) and **Geraldo Perdomo** (65.48), our two most compelling starts. **Taylor Ward** leads the charge as he benefits from a massive 16% park boost against Boyd; despite the moderate 45% rain risk, his leadoff spot and favorable platoon split make him our highest-projected asset today. Similarly, **Geraldo Perdomo** serves as an elite play in a middle-infield slot, taking advantage of a high-ceiling matchup against Germ\u00e1n M\u00e1rquez. While his projected #2 spot is technically pending, the +19% SP skill adjustment against the struggling M\u00e1rquez makes him nearly a \"must-start\" regardless of his final placement in the order. **Gavin Sheets** also trends toward the top tier (61.65) by capitalizing on an underrated platoon advantage against Zac Gallen, proving that targeting specific favorable matchups is currently outperforming raw base projections.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Drew Gilbert** (36.68), who just missed the cut. While the optimizer likes his situational potential, his projected #8 spot coupled with a -19% SP skill drag against Spencer Miles ultimately suppressed his score below our floor, pushing **Jung Hoo Lee** and **Luis Arraez** into the UTIL and OF2 spots despite their own challenging matchups against Miles. We are also keeping a close watch on **Dylan Beavers** (43.28); while he benefits from a park boost, the combination of a difficult L/L platoon matchup and a 45% rain risk at his venue makes him our most volatile start of the day. We remain cautious with **Cal Raleigh** (51.43) as well, given the -7% SP skill penalty against Meyer, though his status as a primary catcher keeps him in the lineup to ensure we don't bleed categorical volume. As **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, **Will Smith**, and **Lawrence Butler** remain unavailable due to injury or rest, our core depth\u2014including **Tyler Freeman** and **Daulton Varsho**\u2014will need to remain hyper-efficient to maintain our momentum in the SB and AVG categories.",
